# 🔐 Session-Based Authentication vs JWT

Sessions and JWTs can both be used to protect backend resources, but they work differently.

### Session-Based Authentication

With sessions, the server maintains information about the authenticated user.

In this project:

```text
Client
   ↓
POST /session-login
   ↓
Server creates session
   ↓
Session stores user
   ↓
Client receives session cookie
   ↓
GET /session-protected
   ↓
Server checks session
   ↓
Allow / Reject
```

The important idea is:

```text
Authentication State
        ↓
     Server
        ↓
     Session
```

---

### JWT-Based Authentication

With JWT authentication, the server creates a signed token containing information about the user.

In this project:

```text
Client
   ↓
POST /jwt-login
   ↓
Server creates JWT
   ↓
Client receives token
   ↓
Client sends token
   ↓
GET /jwt-protected
   ↓
Server verifies token
   ↓
Allow / Reject
```

The important idea is:

```text
Authentication State
        ↓
       JWT
        ↓
      Client
```

