# 🧠 Key Concepts

## 1. Stateful Authentication

Session-based authentication is commonly described as stateful because authentication information is maintained on the server.

```text
Client
   ↓
Session ID
   ↓
Server
   ↓
Session Data
```

The server needs access to the session information when processing the request.

---

## 2. Stateless Authentication

JWT authentication is commonly described as stateless because the authentication information needed to verify the token can be contained in the token itself.

```text
Client
   ↓
JWT
   ↓
Server
   ↓
Verify Signature
   ↓
Allow / Reject
```

The server does not need to maintain the same type of session record for every authenticated request.

---

## 3. Token Signing

The JWT is signed using:

```javascript
SECRET_KEY
```

The signature helps the server determine whether the token can be verified using the expected secret.

---

## 4. Token Expiration

This project configures the JWT to expire:

```javascript
{
  expiresIn: "1h"
}
```

That means the token is intended to remain valid for one hour.

After expiration, verification should fail and the client would need to authenticate again or use a refresh-token mechanism in a more complete system.

---

## 5. Protected Routes

Both authentication methods protect resources.

Session:

```text
/session-protected
```

JWT:

```text
/jwt-protected
```

The basic pattern is:

```text
Request
   ↓
Authentication Check
   ↓
Authenticated?
   │
   ├── Yes → Protected Resource
   │
   └── No → 401
```

